WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.180
Oh, hi.

9
00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.640
We're going to make a real splash today.

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.180
A little splish splash.

11
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.260
We're having a pool party.

12
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.200
It is Season 8, Episode 12, written by Owen Ellickson.

13
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.080
This was a new writer to our staff.

14
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.300
This is his first episode.

15
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.420
And directed by, action, Charles McDougal.

16
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.340
Here is your summary.

17
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:51.260
In the midst of his divorce, Robert California throws a pool party for his employees at his soon-to-be-sold house.

18
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:58.800
Meanwhile, Jim tries to sneak out early, and Erin enlists Dwight to flirt with her to try to make Andy jealous.

19
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.640
Oh, my goodness.

20
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.720
The cringe factor on their flirting was, I don't know, DEFCON 5.

21
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.700
Fast fact number one today is a location breakdown.

22
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.020
We got a lot of fan mail about this crazy house.

23
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.340
Andy C.

24
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.720
from Durham, North Carolina, who said,

25
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.080
This house is incredible.

26
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.120
Is it real?

27
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.300
And from Sarah in Kingwood, Texas, who said,

28
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.820
Was each room shown, including the pool, all one house and location?

29
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.500
Thanks for your questions.

30
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.180
We got all the deets.

31
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.060
We did.

32
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.340
Jenna reached out to Steve Burgess, and I reached out to Matt Flynn.

33
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:49.240
Steve Burgess said that pool party was one of the most complicated and expensive episodes we ever shot of The Office,

34
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.140
and it all came down to locations.

35
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:59.100
Steve said they spent 12 hours driving around in a van looking at different houses with swimming pools,

36
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.940
and they could not find one that worked.

37
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.040
So they ended up renting two different houses.

38
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:04.420
Yep.

39
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.180
One for when they go on the house tour, and one for the swimming scenes.

40
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.040
Yes.

41
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.400
I went to the house for the swimming scenes.

42
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.460
I didn't do the tour.

43
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.540
You know, Angela Martin doesn't go on the tour, so I have my own personal stories I'm going to share about that house.

44
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.180
But Matt Flynn, who was now our production designer, he shared some great nuggets, lady.

45
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:25.700
Okay.

46
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.480
First of all, he said the script called for very specific things,

47
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.140
that the character of James Spader was extremely wealthy and somewhat mysterious

48
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:43.620
and should have a grand residence, which included an indoor pool, as one might find around Scranton, Pennsylvania.

49
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.540
And then his email to me, he said, so let's start there.

50
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.100
Locations scoured San Fernando Valley, like Steve said.

51
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.520
It was very hard to meet all these demands.

52
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.280
And then they heard about this house off of Mulholland Drive

53
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.280
that was owned by a famous sports legend that had an indoor pool.

54
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.760
They were like, oh, this is going to check all the boxes.

55
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.440
It was not available.

56
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.100
And they made multiple offers over and over again, and they said no.

57
00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.040
So they had to continue their search.

58
00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:26.920
Well, eventually, they found two houses, and fast fact number two is a breakdown of those houses.

59
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.620
So let's start with the one they used for the house on the tour.

60
00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.060
This was shot at a real house in Encino, California.

61
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.440
Steve Burgess said the houses on the street were all incredible.

62
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.680
The Jackson family had a house that was like one block away from this house.

63
00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.440
But we took out the owner's real furniture, and we put in our own.

64
00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:49.900
Steve said the furniture was like so expensive, we could not afford to mess up their real furniture.

65
00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.920
We added all of the neon that was in the media room.

66
00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:54.720
That was us.

67
00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.540
And I guess there was a giant painting, like the size of an entire wall.

68
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.460
Steve sent me a picture of it, and we couldn't remove it.

69
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.060
So we had to rope it off so that we didn't damage it.

70
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.180
It looks like a museum, like how you rope things off in a museum.

71
00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.140
I guess this painting was worth a fortune.

72
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.560
There was also an outdoor pool at this house.

73
00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.660
It was in the backyard, and we had to avoid showing it out any of the windows so that it

74
00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.360
wouldn't ruin the story that Robert had an indoor pool.

75
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:26.720
Yes, Matt said this Encino house was a fantastic 1980s-era mansion.

76
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:33.960
He said it had a brutalist design, a sophisticated and elegant board-poured concrete residence

77
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:40.440
offset with panels of smooth Venetian plaster panels and other high-end elements, but no

78
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.080
indoor pool.

79
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.460
The house with the pool was in South Pasadena.

80
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:46.740
But here's the thing.

81
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.360
It also was not an indoor pool.

82
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.140
It was sort of like an indoor-outdoor pool.

83
00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:01.000
Steve sent me a picture of this as well, and it was sort of like half outdoors with

84
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.780
a big door that led to a partial indoor pool area.

85
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:14.600
So he said that our crews built a bunch of fake walls in order to enclose the whole pool.

86
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.200
It took like 24 hours a day for three days.

87
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:23.480
We also had to lay down a complicated electrical grid so that we could light the room.

88
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.220
Steve said every light had something called a shock blocker so that if something fell in

89
00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.420
the pool, it would cut off power before anyone got hurt.

90
00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.980
But this was a massive construction job.

91
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:34.280
Massive.

92
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.120
Okay, so I have some photos I took from this episode that I will share in our stories.

93
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.860
One photo I took is just everyone hanging out, and you can see all the lights that they had

94
00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.720
to put around the pool when we were filming.

95
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.720
They are right next to the pool.

96
00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.960
So yeah, it's a good thing they had the, what were they called?

97
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:52.140
Shock blockers.

98
00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.620
Yeah, the shock blockers.

99
00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:59.480
Here's what Matt said about the Pasadena house, which by the way, was 30 miles away from the

100
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.300
Encino house.

101
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.820
So imagine the back and forth.

102
00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.100
He said this house was spectacular.

103
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.300
It was built in the 1960s and had a Mondrian color palette.

104
00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.760
He said these two houses did not match in any way.

105
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.000
Oh my gosh.

106
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.420
So they had to decide which look of the house they wanted.

107
00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:21.560
They picked the look of the Encino house, and then they had to incorporate that design

108
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.440
onto the Pasadena house.

109
00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:30.240
That included cladding all the existing interior surfaces with walls that resembled the board

110
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.600
poured concrete and the same Venetian panels from the Encino house.

111
00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.280
And all of this had to happen within one week.

112
00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.080
That is bonkers.

113
00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:40.840
Bonkers.

114
00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:46.400
And he said Pasadena has very strict rules regarding any production company.

115
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.200
Interrupting their, quote, placid environment.

116
00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:57.160
I know this because we used to shoot splitting up together at a house in Pasadena, and they

117
00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.020
have very strict rules about when you can enter and leave neighborhoods.

118
00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:01.640
Yes.

119
00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.540
They call it taillights out.

120
00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.500
That means I need to see your taillights leaving as of 10 p.m.

121
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.900
He said all the construction crews were doubled.

122
00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.320
They doubled the size of the crews to meet this challenge.

123
00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:22.680
He said, and I quote, it was a real cluster F, but we had such limited hours to work.

124
00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.860
And he said there were all of these parking restrictions in Pasadena in the neighborhood.

125
00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.080
I remember that, too.

126
00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:34.260
So I remember this because our trailers were very far away, and we had to be shuttled back

127
00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.640
and forth, and so did the crews.

128
00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:40.640
They parked our trailers at a shopping center, like a good distance from this neighborhood.

129
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:47.360
Yes, they have rules about parking trailers in neighborhoods, like cast changing rooms and

130
00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.700
trailers and big trucks.

131
00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.160
Matt wanted to give a shout out to the construction, scenic painters, and set decorating crews because

132
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.480
he said they were absolute masters at their game and produced a glorious finished environment

133
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.580
for this particularly quirky episode.

134
00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:07.100
He was told at the time, this single episode's construction's costs were more than the first

135
00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.640
three or four seasons' costs combined.

136
00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:10.620
Yes.

137
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.840
Matt said he's also been very grateful to the episode's director, Charles McDougal, for his

138
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:16.280
support.

139
00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:21.920
He said once they wrapped, Charles gave him a very fine bottle of wine and a card which

140
00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.700
read, we had no right to be able to get this episode shot last week.

141
00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.300
You made the right call on the locations and designed them brilliantly.

142
00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.640
Thanks so much for all of the good work.

143
00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.120
That's Charles McDougal right there.

144
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:34.560
That's Charles McDougal.

145
00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:37.660
He works you hard, but he appreciates you.

146
00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:38.360
He sure does.

147
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.440
Well, next up is Fast Fact 3, which I titled, Cringiest Episode.

148
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.580
You brought this up earlier, Angela.

149
00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.720
We got a fan mail flurry on this topic, actually.

150
00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.740
I'm going to read one from Melody S.

151
00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.260
in Olive Branch, Mississippi, who said,

152
00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.280
I work in an office and thought how awkward it would be for me to be in a bathing suit with

153
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.440
all of my co-workers who I only see at the office.

154
00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:08.440
Was it weird and awkward for everyone to be in bathing suits in front of their co-workers?

155
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:09.480
Yes.

156
00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.140
Yes, it was.

157
00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.240
I mean, we weren't the cast of Baywatch.

158
00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.720
Okay, fine.

159
00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.520
Baywatch, you hire me day one.

160
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.820
I'm in a bathing suit with you.

161
00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:20.680
That's our outfit.

162
00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:21.660
That's what we wear.

163
00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.980
I know what you look like in a bathing suit from day one.

164
00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:28.220
Not the case when you work at a paper company.

165
00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.680
So, you know, I was trading messages with Ellie Kemper about this episode,

166
00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.860
and she sent in some audio clips, and the first one that she sent in,

167
00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.780
without me even asking her this question, was on this topic.

168
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:42.020
Yeah.

169
00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:42.940
Let's listen.

170
00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.160
Hi, Jenna and Angela.

171
00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:47.180
This is Ellie.

172
00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.060
Okay, I have some thoughts about pool party.

173
00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.920
First of all, I loved that episode.

174
00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.540
I don't know if you did, but I loved being on location.

175
00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.400
I'm pretty sure we were in someone's actual house, right, with an indoor pool.

176
00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:00.260
So that was a ton of fun.

177
00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.340
I will tell you, I don't know if you had a similar reaction,

178
00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.820
but when we did the table read the week before,

179
00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.860
we found out we all had to be in swimsuits for a week.

180
00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.600
They gave us like five days notice, five days heads up.

181
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.000
Okay, be swimsuit ready.

182
00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.900
I panicked because it was like, I'm pretty sure it was, I don't know, winter.

183
00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:27.840
It was not a time where you were thinking, oh, I'm going to be out on the beach in a swimsuit.

184
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.680
So I remember like for three days, I did like push-ups and then stopped.

185
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.660
Anyway, so that got me swimsuit ready.

186
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:37.400
Yes.

187
00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:38.580
I mean, that's bonkers.

188
00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.300
That's something like, if you do a movie, you're like,

189
00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.880
here's everything that's going to be required of me for this movie.

190
00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:49.680
But when you're on a TV show, you learn the week before what's required of you the next week.

191
00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.100
Yeah.

192
00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.100
So there's no prep time.

193
00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.260
I mean, we'll get into it.

194
00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:57.160
I had to wear a fake preggers belly in a bathing suit.

195
00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.660
I'm going to get into it later.

196
00:10:58.900 --> 00:10:59.260
Okay.

197
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.720
Well, you know, there are so many lists of the cringiest office episodes,

198
00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.320
and Pool Party never makes the liSt.

199
00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:11.260
However, I have to say after watching it, this one has moved to the top of the list for me.

200
00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.180
I think before this, it was maybe Prince Family Paper.

201
00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.700
I found Garden Party quite cringey.

202
00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.180
I mean, Scott's Tots is moving down the list for me.

203
00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:21.400
That's saying something.

204
00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:21.940
I know.

205
00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.140
I'm with you on the cringe factor on this one.

206
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:28.600
There's a few moments that I was like, wait, what?

207
00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.080
Like I forgot happened.

208
00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.940
I found an article on CheatSheet.com titled,

209
00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.540
How the Office Fans Feel About Pool Party.

210
00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.600
And it came out after this episode was released.

211
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.940
I guess NBC's The Office Instagram had posted a clip from Pool Party,

212
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.500
and fans quickly took to the comments to name the episode

213
00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.180
the weirdest office episode in history.

214
00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:52.640
One comment read,

215
00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:55.140
This episode was dot dot dot.

216
00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:56.580
It was.

217
00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:58.760
Let's just leave it at that.

218
00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.460
I think that sums it up.

219
00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.100
That's pretty good.

220
00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.820
You know, I have a confession about this episode.

221
00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.700
This episode, this was my first time watching it.

222
00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.800
I did not watch it when it aired.

223
00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:13.720
Oh, yeah?

224
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.980
Yeah, I experienced the dot dot dot for the very first time.

225
00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.000
It is truly cringey.

226
00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:20.480
I know.

227
00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:22.540
You know, I had read the table draft.

228
00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.700
They still sent it to me on my maternity leave.

229
00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.380
And, you know, I actually offered to call in for this episode.

230
00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.860
I thought Jim and Pam could have a phone call,

231
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.780
like maybe when Jim is trying to leave Robert's house.

232
00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.880
Yeah, like Pam's like, hey, where are you at?

233
00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.560
Yes, when are you going to get home?

234
00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.780
I thought that would put more pressure on him.

235
00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:41.180
But the network said no.

236
00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.060
They said you couldn't call in?

237
00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:44.940
No, because then they'd have to pay me.

238
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:45.460
Ew.

239
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:46.320
Yeah.

240
00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:48.420
But don't you think that would have been good?

241
00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:49.040
I do.

242
00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:52.020
If she's like, instead of just having no Pam in this episode?

243
00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:52.780
I don't know.

244
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.840
But no, they didn't go for it.

